# TEMPLATE ONLY — this file IS committed. Never put a real value in it.
# Copy it to .env (which is gitignored) and fill that in instead; the CLI
# reads .env, never this file, so a value typed here does nothing except leak.

# Gemini — used only for translation and drafting editorial copy.
# Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=



# Which Gemini model to use. Blank = the current default in ai.py. Google
# retires ids on a schedule and a retired one 404s ("no longer available to
# new users") — set this rather than editing code when that happens.
GEMINI_MODEL=

# Future IPO data source. Leave blank to stay on the manual YAML provider.
# See backend/ipopulse/providers/api.py for the contract to implement.
IPOPULSE_API_BASE=
IPOPULSE_API_KEY=

# ── Google Sheets write-back ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed to push data INTO a sheet. Reading one needs nothing here:
# publish it as CSV (File → Share → Publish to web → CSV) and hand the URL
# straight to `ipopulse import`.
#
# Path to the service-account key file — the PATH, never the JSON itself.
# Keep the file outside the repo, or add it to .gitignore: only .env is
# ignored today, so a stray key.json sitting next to it would be committed.
GOOGLE_SHEETS_KEY=

# The long id from the sheet URL:
#   https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/<THIS_PART>/edit
GOOGLE_SHEETS_ID=

# Which tab to write to. Blank = the first one.
GOOGLE_SHEETS_TAB=

# Three things must be true before a write succeeds, and none of them live in
# the key file:
#   1. the Sheets API is enabled on the key's GCP project
#   2. the sheet is shared with the key's client_email as EDITOR, not Viewer
#   3. the code requests scope .../auth/spreadsheets, not .../spreadsheets.readonly

# ── Trigger panel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Password for http://localhost:8000/trigger, the manual run page.
# Forgotten it? Change this line and restart `ipopulse serve` — nothing
# else stores it. Blank disables the panel entirely.
#
# If you host the backend (see render.yaml), this password becomes reachable
# from the internet — use a long random one there, not this local value.
#
# It is also the site's front-door password (js/gate.js) and the key that
# unseals GH_DISPATCH_PAT below, so on a published deployment it is carrying
# three jobs. Long and random there, no exceptions.
IPOPULSE_TRIGGER_PASSWORD=

# ── Narration: which provider ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ordered, comma separated. The first one that is configured and answers wins;
# the rest are fallbacks. Default: gemini,elevenlabs
#
# WHY GEMINI IS FIRST, and it is not about quality. Checked 27 Aug 2026:
#
#   Gemini      the free tier's output MAY be used commercially. The Additional
#               Terms say only "Google won't claim ownership over that content"
#               and describe the Services as being for "professional or
#               business purposes". Unpaid vs paid changes how GOOGLE USES YOUR
#               DATA, not what you may do with the audio.
#               Speaks English, Hindi AND Telugu. 30 prebuilt voices, no
#               cloning. Style is steered by prompt, not by sliders.
#
#   ElevenLabs  the free plan grants NO commercial licence at all, so for a
#               monetised channel it needs a paid plan. In exchange it can
#               clone your own voice, which playbook §3.1 makes the safer
#               branch for finance narration.
#
# So on a strictly-free setup Gemini is the only one of the two that works.
# The price is not money: unpaid Gemini quota means prompts and outputs are
# used to improve Google's products and "human reviewers may read, annotate,
# and process" them. These scripts are built from public market data, so that
# is an acceptable trade — but it is a real one. Do not send anything private
# through the free tier.
#
# Also worth knowing: Gemini TTS is in PREVIEW. Its own docs warn that quality
# drifts past a few minutes of output, that it occasionally returns text
# instead of audio (a 500 — retried automatically here), and that preview
# models carry tighter rate limits than stable ones.
#
# Compare them by ear before settling:
#   ipopulse voice --compare --lang te "ఇది ఒక పరీక్ష"
IPOPULSE_VOICE_PROVIDERS=

# ── Narration (Gemini) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No key of its own: it reuses GEMINI_API_KEY from the top of this file, and
# the same free quota as the text calls. Nothing else is required.
#
# One of the 30 prebuilt voices. Charon is "Informative", which is the register
# these scripts are written in; Rasalgethi is the other informative option, and
# Alnilam or Kore read firmer. Suffix it per language — GEMINI_TTS_VOICE_TE — if
# one voice suits Telugu better than English.
GEMINI_TTS_VOICE=

# Defaults to gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts.
GEMINI_TTS_MODEL=

# Overrides the built-in director's note that sets the delivery. Gemini TTS is
# prompt-steered rather than parameter-steered, so this text IS the voice
# direction. Keep it describing a REGISTER and never a résumé: playbook §3.1
# bars an AI persona presenting itself as a human financial expert, and the
# scripts had their tenure claims removed for exactly that reason.
GEMINI_TTS_STYLE=

# ── Narration (ElevenLabs) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Lets the studio generate the voiceover for the reel on screen and
# record it into the video in one pass, instead of pasting the script into
# elevenlabs.io and syncing it in CapCut afterwards.
#
# The key stays HERE, server-side. The studio POSTs the script text to
# /api/voice and gets mp3 bytes back; it never sees the key. That is a harder
# line than the GitHub PAT above, which is sealed and shipped: a leaked PAT
# runs the same cron jobs you already run, a leaked voice key runs up a bill.
#
# NOT a GitHub secret. Actions secrets only exist inside a workflow run, and
# /api/voice is not a workflow — it is the backend serving the studio. So this
# key lives in this file locally, and in the HOST's environment variables
# (Render's dashboard, say) if you host the backend. Adding it to GitHub
# Secrets is harmless and inert: nothing would ever read it.
#
# Billed per character of input, so:
#   - every result is cached forever, keyed on the exact text + voice +
#     settings. Re-generating an unchanged script costs nothing.
#   - ELEVENLABS_MONTHLY_CHAR_CAP is a local tripwire, checked before each
#     call. It counts what this machine sent, not your real balance.
#
# ── READ THIS BEFORE PICKING A PLAN ──────────────────────────────────────
# Checked against elevenlabs.io on 27 August 2026.
#
# **The free plan carries no commercial licence.** ElevenLabs' own help centre:
# "The free plan does not include a commercial license and cannot be used for
# any commercial purpose", and "content created outside of a paid subscription
# (before or after) cannot be used commercially". A monetised YouTube channel is
# a commercial purpose, so free-tier audio cannot be used here at all — this is
# the licence, not a quota technicality. All paid plans do include one.
#
# Prices, per 1,000 characters:  v3 $0.10 · multilingual v2 $0.10 · flash $0.05
# Characters included per month: Free 10k · Starter $6 60k · Creator $22 220k
#                                Pro $99 990k
#
# Do the arithmetic before you switch this on. A 6-10 minute long-form
# narration is roughly 5,000-8,500 characters; times three languages, times
# thirty days, is somewhere near 600k-900k a month. That is Pro territory, or
# pay-as-you-go. Measure your own scripts rather than trusting that range —
# `ipopulse voice --budget` counts what you actually send.
#
# ── Several keys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This variable accepts MORE THAN ONE key, comma or space separated, and
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY_2 … _5 are read as well. They are tried in order and the
# next one takes over when a key is rate limited, revoked, or out of quota.
#
# What that is good for: a paid key with a paid spare, and riding out the
# concurrency limit (2 requests on Free, 5 on Creator).
#
# What it cannot do is manufacture quota. Three free plans is 30,000 characters
# — about one day of three-language output — and none of it is licensed for a
# monetised channel anyway. Also worth knowing: a cloned voice belongs to ONE
# workspace, so keys from separate accounts answer 404 for a voice id they do
# not have, unless the voice is cloned in each.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY_2=
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY_3=

# Which voice. `ipopulse voice --voices` lists what the key can use and marks
# the one set here. Use YOUR OWN cloned voice: playbook §3.1 is a hard
# monetisation bar, and a stock voice presenting as a knowledgeable adviser on
# finance is the named risky case. A clone of your own voice is not.
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=

# Defaults to eleven_multilingual_v2. Worth keeping for English and Hindi: the
# English-only models mispronounce Indian company names even in the English
# scripts.
ELEVENLABS_MODEL=

# ── Per-language overrides ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both of the above take a _EN / _HI / _TE suffix, and the suffixed one wins.
# `ipopulse voice --plan` prints what each language resolves to.
#
# TWO REASONS THIS EXISTS:
#
# 1. TELUGU NEEDS A DIFFERENT MODEL — settled, not a guess. Per
#    elevenlabs.io/docs/models on 27 Aug 2026, eleven_multilingual_v2 supports
#    29 languages: it has Hindi, it has TAMIL, and it has no Telugu.
#    eleven_flash_v2_5 adds only hu/no/vi, so it has no Telugu either. Only
#    eleven_v3 (70+ languages) lists Telugu (tel).
#
#    So the default for `te` is already eleven_v3 and you should not need to set
#    this. Two consequences worth knowing: v3 caps a request at 5,000
#    characters where multilingual v2 allows 10,000, so a long Telugu script has
#    to be split where the English one does not; and `ipopulse voice --plan`
#    prints an "ok" column that refuses in advance if a language is on a model
#    that cannot say it.
#
#    Still verify by ear once — an unsupported language returns confident
#    nonsense rather than an error, so a 200 proves nothing:
#
#      ipopulse voice --lang te "ఇది ఒక పరీక్ష" --out te-test.mp3
#
# 2. A CLONE CARRIES ITS SOURCE ACCENT. One voice cloned from your English
#    speech will read Telugu intelligibly and still sound like an English
#    speaker reading Telugu, which a Telugu audience hears instantly. So the
#    voice is overridable per language too.
#
#    The tension is real and yours to settle: §3.1 makes your own clone the
#    policy-safe branch and a stock voice the risky one, but your own clone may
#    be the weaker read in Telugu. Own clone everywhere is the conservative
#    answer; a native stock voice is the better listen and needs the AI
#    disclosure ticked and no credential claims in the script.
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID_HI=
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID_TE=
ELEVENLABS_MODEL_TE=

# Local spend tripwire, characters per calendar month. Default 100000.
# ── how it is read: stability, style, speed ────────────────────────────────
#
# Three knobs, each taking a NUMBER or the word `auto`, and each overridable
# per language (ELEVENLABS_STYLE_TE beats ELEVENLABS_STYLE).
#
#   stability  HIGH is consistent; LOW lets the model act. It is also what
#              lets it improvise, which on a line full of figures risks the
#              voice saying a different number than the card shows.
#   style      the exaggeration dial. 0.0 is none at all.
#   speed      1.0 is the voice's natural pace.
#
# `auto` derives the field from the script being spoken (speech.delivery).
# The governing rule is the opposite of the obvious one: STABILITY RISES WITH
# NUMERIC DENSITY. A figure-heavy reel is read straight because there is
# something in it to get factually wrong; a verdict reel is argument, so it
# gets the latitude. Density is scored against a per-LANGUAGE reference,
# because Hindi and Telugu say the same numbers in a third of the words and so
# run 2-3x denser than English — one global threshold would peg every Indic
# reel at maximum and lose the variation worth having.
#
# Measured across 24 IPOs x 6 reels, `auto` produces roughly:
#     Daily GMP      stability .68  style .12  speed .97   (dense)
#     Final Verdict  stability .53  style .22  speed 1.02  (sparse)
#
# Leave a field blank for the documented default (0.65 / 0.0 / 0.95).
ELEVENLABS_STABILITY=auto
ELEVENLABS_STYLE=auto
ELEVENLABS_SPEED=auto
ELEVENLABS_SIMILARITY=

ELEVENLABS_MONTHLY_CHAR_CAP=

# ── Running Actions from the studio ───────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. A fine-grained GitHub PAT so the published site's "Run job" panel
# can dispatch .github/workflows/schedule.yml without you pasting a token
# every session. Create it at github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens with:
#
#   Repository access   only this repo
#   Permissions         Actions -> Read and write, nothing else
#   Expiration          short; re-issue rather than extend
#
# Set it here for a local `ipopulse config`, and as the GH_DISPATCH_PAT
# repository SECRET for the publish workflow.
#
# How it reaches the browser without leaking: `ipopulse config` encrypts it
# (AES-256-GCM, key from PBKDF2 over IPOPULSE_TRIGGER_PASSWORD with its own
# random salt) and writes only the ciphertext into frontend/js/config.js.
# gate.js decrypts it when you type the site password. The plaintext token is
# never written to any file, and publish.yml's leak scan fails the deploy if
# one ever is.
#
# What that costs you, stated plainly: the site password now also protects a
# token that can run Actions on this repo, and it can be attacked offline from
# the published ciphertext. Long random password, tightly scoped token.
# Blank means the studio asks for a token by hand instead.
GH_DISPATCH_PAT=

# ── Hosting the trigger API ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed if you want to run jobs from the PUBLISHED site. Pages serves
# files, so nothing there can run a job and a browser will not let an https
# page call http://localhost — the backend needs a public https address.
#
# Set this to the deployed URL and `ipopulse config` writes it into
# frontend/js/config.js, which is what tells the site where to call.
# Blank (the normal case) means the site falls back to GitHub Actions.
#
# NOT the same as IPOPULSE_API_BASE above: that one is a future IPO *data
# source* for providers/api.py.
IPOPULSE_TRIGGER_API=

# Browser origins allowed to call /api/*, comma separated. Required when the
# API is hosted, since the site is on a different origin. Never a wildcard —
# these endpoints start jobs.
IPOPULSE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Set to 1 only behind a proxy that sets X-Forwarded-For (Render, Fly, a CDN).
# The failed-login lockout counts per client address; behind a proxy every
# request shares one, so without this five bad guesses lock out everybody.
# Leave blank when directly exposed — the header is caller-supplied, and
# trusting it then would let anyone reset their own lockout at will.
IPOPULSE_TRUST_PROXY=
YOUTUBE_STUDIO_URL=